%erg 0.1
Title: Docs: one upgrade recipe for claims; init refreshes docs, not data
Created: 2026-11-09
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-11-09T11:56Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---
## Context
2026-11-09, after #393 made claims required. integration.md and the CHANGELOG gave the upgrade as
`erg sync`, `erg init`, `erg migrate --claims`, commit, `erg install --hooks`. `erg init` is not
needed for claims: it refreshes the shipped docs (tickets/AGENTS.md, .ergrc), and `erg check` warns
until it runs. Tested on a store set up by the 0306 binary: migrate and check work without init.
The author asked why init is needed and decided not to make `erg sync` refresh the docs (one
command, once per release, for one person; the drift warning already names `erg init`).

A planned `erg sync` hint ("run migrate") was dropped: the first upgrade to a claims release runs
the OLD binary's sync, which cannot print it, and the new binary's first command (check, new, init)
already names `erg migrate --claims`.

## Design
Docs only (integration.md "ID claims" and "Keeping a store current", CHANGELOG upgrade note):
- Who does what. Whoever imports the release, once: `erg sync --upstream`, review,
  `tickets/erg migrate --claims tickets`, `tickets/erg init` (docs), commit. Every clone: `git pull`
  brings the binary, docs and claims; then `erg install --hooks` once (hooks are per clone).
- A branch cut before the migration: after rebasing, run `erg migrate --claims tickets` again; it
  adds only the missing claims.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] integration.md and CHANGELOG give the recipe above; no doc lists `erg init` as a claims step.
- [ ] The old-branch rerun is documented.
- [ ] `make check` passes.
